testing disaster recovery and migrating  workloads between sites are the primary  advantages of site recovery manager on  google cloud vmware engine  i'm darren schmitz with vmware and in  this final video of the series we will  configure and test a recovery plan  migrate the example application to the  other site and perform a reprotect  in the previous video the replications  had just finished transferring the data  and have transitioned to a green okay  status  as discussed previously the protection  group auto created with our replications  contains our three example virtual  machines for app one  and we can also switch to the recovery  plan to customize it  one of the helpful features of the  recovery plan is the ability to suspend  non-critical vms at the recovery site  a common example of this is where a site  is used for development and the virtual  machines can be suspended to free  resources for the production workloads  when testing the plan test networks will  be created to isolate the workloads and  prevent ip address conflicts  this also gives administrators an  opportunity to non-disruptively test  upgrades or patches on the workload  while the test copies of the vms are  running  finally the recovery plan has five  priority groups that vms can be placed  into for a prescribed startup order  for example if ubuntu serve001 is a  database dns or active directory server  that needs to start prior to the others  it can be assigned into priority group  2.  before we start the test let's examine  what each site looks like before and  after  the primary site has three application  servers running while the secondary site  has the placeholder vms  placeholder vms indicate those vms are  being protected at the primary site  initiating the test opens a wizard  asking whether replicating recent  changes to the recovery site is desired  unchecking this box presents the  opportunity to simulate a failure where  the primary site is unreachable  as the test starts it will proceed  through each of the recovery steps to  simulate what it will do when the plan  is run  it will synchronize and configure the  storage create the test networks and  start powering on the virtual machines  in a few moments an admin can inspect  and test the application to verify the  plan completed as expected  flipping over to the secondary site the  placeholder vms have been started and  added to the isolated test network  switching to the primary site the  application is still running  uninterrupted  as a feature for the recovery plan a  history is recorded to examine the  carried out tasks more closely  for example a report can be generated  and provided to an auditor for  compliance reasons or could even be used  to estimate how long it will take to  fail an application to the opposite site  once we are satisfied with the test the  cleanup will return the environment back  to its original state  in a few moments the recovery plan can  be readjusted and run again and again  until the expected result is obtained  now that the test was successful the  recovery plan can now be run  after acknowledging the warning a  planned migration will attempt a final  sync of the data between sites whereas  the disaster recovery will start the  plan with everything in its current  state  running the plan will gracefully shut  down the virtue machines configure the  storage and power on the application in  the same prescribed order  when finished switching to site 1 shows  the virtual machines are powered off and  left off in case a rollback is needed  at site 2 the virtual machines for app 1  are now successfully running  once an admin has established that the  application is operating correctly at  the other site a reprotect can be  initiated  a reprotect uses the reverse mappings to  reconfigure the replication to flow back  in the opposite direction  acknowledging the warning and clicking  finish the reprotect is started  in a short amount of time the process  completes and returns to the recovery  plan  now that the application is running at  site 2 the placeholder vms are now shown  in the inventory at site 1.  this indicates that the application is  now ready to be gracefully migrated or  failed back to site 1 when needed  this concludes the site recovery manager  on google cloud vmware engine video  series thank you for watching  to learn more about google cloud vmware  engine visit our site at  cloud.google.com vmware dash engine  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]
 

